Z||000005||Oreif||12-12-2000||05:16 PM||||The RCS (Radar Cross Section) of a fiero is about the size of a motorcycle. Even a Corvette's RCS is slightly bigger than a motorcycle. One of the many reasons it takes longer to pick up a fiero is the radiator is angled and the engine is in back. The average range of a speed radar is about 2500 feet. A fiero can be picked up at 1800 feet. I don't remember which magazine it was in but they did a study on which cars were "stealthier". Also the Fiero can be picked up better from the rear due to the flat back window, and trunk/engine compartments. As for speed accuracy, The Fiero and corvette generally read up to 5% lower. (depends on radar type, freq, and calibration) This is due to the radar signal has to pass thru the plastic/fiberglass then get reflected back thru. This screws up the freq shift of the radar unit. To calculate the speed of a vehicle the radar sends out a signal at the specified freq. (X, K, Ka bands) The radar "waves" bounce off the vehicle. As the vehicle travels the waves get closer together creating the freq shift in the pulse rate. (Doppler effect) This difference is calculated out to give the speed of the target. In K band the shift is 2.5mhz per mile per hour. So if you had a transmitter on your car that transmitted K band and pulsed the output at 137.5mhz and supplied enough power to override the police radar unit. The radar gun would read 55mph.<BR>(I have worked with radar units for many years). Also "Instant-on" radar won't work on a Fiero or corvette if the radar unit is toggled off and on quickly.  To build a jamming unit would cost about $200. But it would be highly illegal since the FCC has to approve all GHz transmitters. (X, K and Ka bands are in the GHz range) If you were caught with such a device, the FCC could imprison you for 10 years and fine you $10,000.<BR>This ends today's lesson on radars, Tomorrow we'll learn about the Laser speed detection devices.<p>[This message has been edited by Oreif (edited 12-12-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000006||DaRkLoRD||12-12-2000||05:16 PM||||Basically anything that reduces flat surfaces on your vehicle will reduce the effect of radar. another solution devised by Lockheed-Martin in the late 1970s, was the use of some Russian formulas for dispersing and reflecting radar waves away from the source.. this was used on an experimental aircraft, in conjunction with a special radar absorbing material, the called "Have Blue" (the project was, not the aircraft itself). This was later put into service as the F117-A Nighthawk stealth fighter.<P>The other option is active radar jamming... you need the frequency range of the police radar, and then you broadcast this signal 180* out of phase, which cancels out the radar waves hitting your vehicle. Unfortunately, this sets off other vehicle's radar detectors, and also any anti-radar detector technology the police are using. In any case, a component called a "gunn diode" can be used to build this device. They're found in supermarket automatic door sensors, and can often be found in electronics surplus stores..<P>Back in the early 1940s, the US Navy attempted an ambitious project to make ships invisible to enemy radar. This project was called "Project Rainbow", but was cancelled shortly after the first test run, due to complications with the equipment which resulted in several crew member deaths relating to the extremely high energy magnetic fields used to surround the ship.<P>btw, I've studied radar systems a bit, back when I was really into the stealth fighter etc.. but there's technology way better than the Nighthawk now... that was developed in 1979.. makes you wonder what cool stuff they've come up with in the last 21 years, eh?   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><P>------------------<BR>   <A HREF="mailto:steve@fieroproject.com">steve@fieroproject.com</A>   <BR><A HREF="http://www.fieroproject.com" TARGET=_blank>http://www.fieroproject.com</A><p>[This message has been edited by DaRkLoRD (edited 12-12-2000).]||||reg||3
