Z||000019||DaRkLoRD||12-13-2000||12:09 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by DJRice:<BR><B>I take it you guys have read "Skunk Works" by Ben Rich & Leo Janos.  If you guys have not, I highly recommend it.  <BR></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Unfortunately not... I've known about that book for several years, but I don't have it yet... I wish I had more time to read, and enough money to buy all the books I wanted.<P>------------------<BR>  <A HREF="mailto:steve@fieroproject.com">steve@fieroproject.com</A>  <BR><A HREF="http://www.fieroproject.com" TARGET=_blank>http://www.fieroproject.com</A>||||reg||1
Z||000020||CozmoPooky||12-13-2000||08:47 AM||||I did notice a speed indication difference (radar read 34 MPH and speedo read 36 MPH) consistently on my "driveby" yesterday.  I think this weekend I'll do some further "experiments" using different cars to establish a baseline to compare the Fiero to.  I'll post whatever data I get.  <BR>The SR-71 used special paint, "iron ball", that absorbed radar waves but it was fragile and ugly (for a car, at least).  There are other forms of passive jamming also.  Special absorbent material could be put under the body panels, other highly reflective components could be "cloaked", etc.<BR>I agree, active ECM on cars is really asking for trouble.  Expensive, difficult and if they ever catch you they'll throw away the key.<BR>BTW, "CozmoPooky" is the names of two pets I used to have. In memory...<P>------------------<BR>CozmoPooky<BR>Suncoast Fieros<BR>'87 Duke Coupe<BR>'85 2M6 Targa||||reg||1
