Z||000022||Oreif||12-13-2000||10:10 AM||||There are plate covers that are suppose to hide the plate for camera's and plate covers that are suppose to stop laser. The only way the laser one would be effective would be if it went across the whole bumper.||||reg||1
Z||000023||Will||12-13-2000||10:51 AM||||I've been thinking about this for a while. Carefully placed and angled sheet metal panels in the front could be used to reflect radar away from the cop's receiver. For instance, a piece on the inside of the nose laid back with similar slope could be used to mask the vertical impact beam in front of the radiator, and similar pieces could be used in front of the headlights to keep them from reflecting when down. When they're up they'd be the next best thing to retro-reflectors, though.<BR>     As a resident of Virginia, one of the reamaining states to require a front license plate, I have come up with a solution that my help GT and Aero-notchback owners: get rid of the plate holder and screw the plate down to the bottom of the intake scoop in the fascia. The resultant angle will reflect radar beams vertically and away from the police receiver.<BR>     I also thought about sand-blasting my plates and repainting them in original colors but with non-reflective paint to make them harder to read at night...<BR>     How much would that protect against lasers? What wavelength do police lasers use? It can't be anything visible or they'd have lawsuits out the wazoo.||||reg||1
Z||000024||Oreif||12-13-2000||02:23 PM||||You cannot just reflect the radar with an angled piece of metal behind the nose. The radar wave is about 6' in diameter when it gets to your car. You would reflect some of it but not all of it. The best way to reflect radar is to reflect it onto itself. If you could reflect the radar from side to side many times you would in effect absorb the signal. So a bunch of cones mounted behind the nose made out of aluminum and coated with carbon fiber would be great. The radar would bounce back and forth inside the cone and the carbon fiber would help attenuate the power. <BR>As for laser, The wavelenght is 850nm. about 750nm is the very high end of the visible spectrum. The laser speed detection devices use 2 beams. One hits the front of the car, the second one hits the road. When the second one is crossed by the car the speed is claculated. Simular to timing lights at a drag strip. Hit the first one and the timer starts, hit the second one and the timer stops. Take the time vs. the distance and you have the speed. You need to have a prism set for the correct wavelenght to scatter the laser. The license plate covers are a basic beam splitter and it angles the laser away. but that only works if the laser is pointed at the plate exactly at a 45* angle. The laser range is about 100 yards and never will the unit be at a 45* angle unless you are very close to passing it. By that time it's too late. The nice thing (for us) is laser is very particular about where it is sent from. So the person using it has to see the vehicle and target it thru an eyepiece without anything in the way. It cannot be fired thru a windshield. So you will be able to see him as soon as he sees you. Where as radar he can set it up and be behind a bush or sign and pick a spot. When you pass thru the radar he reads your speed before you can see him.<P>BTW ~ The best way to avoid getting speeding tickets and avoid heavy fines is to...... not speed.   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><p>[This message has been edited by Oreif (edited 12-13-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000025||Oreif||12-13-2000||02:48 PM||||The below link will tell you all that is known about speed detection and what you can and cannot do to aviod it. <A HREF="http://www.copradar.com/preview/chapt7/ch7d1.html" TARGET=_blank>http://www.copradar.com/preview/chapt7/ch7d1.html</A> <P>||||reg||1
Z||000026||ThatRickGuy||12-13-2000||03:12 PM||||Thanks for the Lazer description. I've been curious. but just out of a curiosity since laser is dependent on reflection, could it be possible to get a wax polish that would alter the reflected beam? just curious. And as for the whole ticketing thing, the only time I ever got caught was when the cop followed me for 2 miles.||||reg||1
